a verb names its rebase with IDENT, resolved in this order: an
absolute path to its manifest; the dataset carrying the record,
in full or by the last part of its name; the pre-apply snapshot,
as "snap" or "pool/fs@snap"; a relative path to its manifest;
its run directory under /var/db/zfs_rebase. Two rebases that
answer to one IDENT are refused, never chosen between, and the
record and the header must name each other. --result is a start
flag: a start takes no IDENT, and no verb takes --result.
every flag has both forms, and the two parse to the same run:
  -f, --from SNAP|DS      the side whose changes are replayed (--off-of)
                          on a verb: checked against the header, never
                          the thing that says which rebase it is
  -t, --onto SNAP|DS      the side they go onto (--to); it sets the form
  -r, --result NAME       the name the run makes; a start flag only;
                          a NAME with no slash goes beside onto's dataset
  -p, --permissive-merge  permissive merge; strict is the default
  -v, --verbose           counts and steps on stderr
  -o, --manifest FILE     where the manifest goes, resolution beside it;
                          a start option, and a dry run's
  -V, --verify            a verb: report one rebase and write
                          nothing; it never repairs, and it goes
                          with no flag that starts or moves one
  -q, --quiet             silence the final check's report; a start
                          option, kept in the record for the whole
                          run
  -O, --take-onto         answer every conflict of the skeleton onto
  -F, --take-from         answer every conflict of the skeleton from
  -i, --interactive [CMD] open a child on the resolution at the
                          conflicts gate and read the document back
                          when it exits 0; anything else and the
                          gate stands. CMD is the command that edits
                          it, run as sh -c with the path as its last
                          argument, and its own flags are part of it
                          ("code --wait"); with none the built-in
                          picker opens. A bare word after -i is CMD
                          unless it is the only one on a verb's
                          line, where it is IDENT. This invocation
                          only: say it on each --continue
  -M, --no-merge          stop at the conflicts gate however the
                          resolution is answered; an error past it
  -c, --continue          take the rebase on from the gate it left
  -R, --restart           the result back as onto was, applied again
  -A, --abort             the rebase undone, as if it never happened
  -n, --dry-run           the manifest only, to stdout without -o;
                          --result is ignored
  -u, --allow-unrelated   no derivation of the base, and no pruning;
                          it needs --base, since there is none to derive
  -b, --base SNAP         with --allow-unrelated only: the base
--posix, --build-fixture and --edit-fixture are the project's own
test aids: they are long only and take the first argument position.
